WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:05.140
Speaker 1: We have an absolutely huge bit
of news and a major step forward in the criminal

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:11.539
investigation of Donald Trump, his associates
and his organization, and I am going to go

00:00:11.539 --> 00:00:17.890
back to what is sometimes called the boiling
frog situation, because it really applies

00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:20.850
here in terms of the normalization of what's
going on.

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:25.880
Yesterday, we learned that the Manhattan district
attorney, Cyrus Vance Jr., has convened a

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:33.490
grand jury to decide whether to indict former
President Donald Trump or his associates or

00:00:33.490 --> 00:00:36.490
other elements of the Trump organization.

00:00:36.490 --> 00:00:42.700
This means that the district attorney at least
believes that he has evidence of possible

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:43.700
crimes.

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:47.190
Otherwise, you don't convene a grand jury.

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:52.920
Whether the crime was committed by Trump himself
or someone close to Trump or all of the above,

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:53.920
we don't yet know.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:56.990
And that is exactly what the grand jury will
determine.

00:00:56.990 --> 00:01:02.960
The Washington Post reports that the panel
was convened recently and will sit three days

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:05.300
a week for six months.

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:08.600
This is not super surprising in terms of the
duration.

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:12.230
White collar crimes take a lot of grand jury
time.

00:01:12.230 --> 00:01:17.830
The cases are complicated and typically grand
juries look at more than one matter.

00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:21.930
So it probably isn't all trump all the time
for the period that they will be on the grand

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:22.930
jury.

00:01:22.930 --> 00:01:26.930
Now, as you might remember, Manhattan District
Attorney Cyrus Vance has been investigating

00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:30.840
Donald Trump's pre presidency business dealings
for more than two years now.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:35.770
And part of that was slowed down because while
Donald Trump was president, he was able to

00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:39.260
regularly obstruct Vance obtaining his tax
records.

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:41.600
That stopped when Trump left office.

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:47.409
And those records were directed to be provided
to Cyrus Vance relatively recently.

00:01:47.409 --> 00:01:50.090
And this is an important detail.

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:55.170
This investigation relates to things Trump
and his company did before Trump was president

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:56.180
and not during.

00:01:56.180 --> 00:02:00.960
Now, Vance's office said in court filings
that it was investigating, quote, possibly

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:05.340
extensive and protracted criminal conduct
at the Trump Organization.

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:09.799
This includes tax fraud, insurance fraud,
falsification of business records and a whole

00:02:09.799 --> 00:02:10.799
bunch of other stuff.

00:02:10.799 --> 00:02:15.620
Now, before looking at the details, a grand
jury looking at a former president is a huge

00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:16.620
deal.

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:17.939
It's enormous.

00:02:17.939 --> 00:02:20.099
And we have to understand that.

00:02:20.099 --> 00:02:23.999
We're going to look at some things that former
prosecutor Renato Mariotti said.

00:02:23.999 --> 00:02:26.799
And we're going to talk about, you know, is
Trump likely to ever go to prison?

00:02:26.799 --> 00:02:28.290
The answer is no.

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:33.719
But consider the sort of, you know, the the
boiling frog in the skillet.

00:02:33.719 --> 00:02:35.659
You slowly turn up the temperature.

00:02:35.659 --> 00:02:38.409
It doesn't notice that it's getting burned.

00:02:38.409 --> 00:02:44.879
Consider how far we've come where, as Sharon
GAPA said on Twitter before the presidency,

00:02:44.879 --> 00:02:47.569
the Access Hollywood grabbed him by the You-Know-What
tape.

00:02:47.569 --> 00:02:49.109
Oh, my goodness, this is crazy.

00:02:49.109 --> 00:02:51.329
This is the craziest thing we've ever seen.

00:02:51.329 --> 00:02:56.709
We have a grand jury now considering indictments
of a former president, and it didn't even

00:02:56.709 --> 00:03:00.500
make that big of news like it made news, but
it wasn't that big.

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:05.719
So think about the degree to which things
have slid over the last many years.

00:03:05.719 --> 00:03:10.900
Now, former federal prosecutor Renato Mariotti,
who has been a guest on the show, had a good

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:13.209
Twitter thread about the meaning of all of
this.

00:03:13.209 --> 00:03:19.290
And just a few points that Renato makes one
quote, The mere fact that a special grand

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:23.709
jury was impaneled does not mean that any
particular person will be charged.

00:03:23.709 --> 00:03:27.040
It does not necessarily mean that Trump himself
will be charged.

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:34.639
But it is fair to say that Vance's investigation
is far along and that charges are on the horizon

00:03:34.639 --> 00:03:39.799
does not mean it will be against Trump number
two when it comes to Trump himself.

00:03:39.799 --> 00:03:44.280
In cases like this, it's often about what
you can prove.

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:48.480
Trump knew when you're talking about this
type of fraud and white collar crime and Rinaudo

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:53.219
tweets, quote, Whenever I investigated tax
or fraud cases, the case came down to whether

00:03:53.219 --> 00:03:57.920
we had sufficient proof of the defendant's
knowledge of the false statements contained

00:03:57.920 --> 00:03:59.299
in the documents.

00:03:59.299 --> 00:04:03.010
That state of mind is what distinguishes fraud
from a mere mistake.

00:04:03.010 --> 00:04:07.189
A slip up is not fraud, very similar to perjury.

00:04:07.189 --> 00:04:11.409
People say things that are not true while
under oath in court all the time.

00:04:11.409 --> 00:04:14.030
But why don't we often see perjury charges?

00:04:14.030 --> 00:04:16.479
Because you have to prove they didn't just
make a mistake.

00:04:16.479 --> 00:04:19.160
It wasn't just a mis recollection that they
were really lying.

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:21.669
And state of mind is a big part of that.

00:04:21.669 --> 00:04:28.350
And lastly, Renato tweets, quote, Fraud is
just when you lie to people to get their money.

00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:33.220
So the case usually comes down to whether
the defendant knew the statements in the documents

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:34.350
were false.

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:38.370
It's easier to prove knowledge for people
involved in preparing or authorizing the documents.

00:04:38.370 --> 00:04:43.720
Like Weissenberg, Weisenberg was one of the
top Trump organization people.

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:48.349
And then in terms of expectations and this
matches what I've been saying for some time,

00:04:48.349 --> 00:04:52.340
Rinaudo tweets, quote, Don't be surprised
if people like Weissenberg are their initial

00:04:52.340 --> 00:04:55.230
target to induce him or others to flip.

00:04:55.230 --> 00:04:58.310
Someone like Trump or his children will only
have liability if they.

00:04:58.310 --> 00:05:04.070
Knew of the false statements and participated
in the scheme in some way or moved it forward

00:05:04.070 --> 00:05:10.340
somehow, so this doesn't change any of the
things we've said, Trump maybe would be charged.

00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:12.900
No way that this leads to Trump going to prison.

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:18.570
But but but if Trump were a normal person,
I would probably be saying this guy's going

00:05:18.570 --> 00:05:19.570
to prison.

00:05:19.570 --> 00:05:20.570
They've got this guy.

00:05:20.570 --> 00:05:26.490
And what I mean by that is think back to the
Michael Cohen indictment and and conviction.

00:05:26.490 --> 00:05:33.879
Michael Cohen names Trump in the case in which
Michael Cohen went to prison for his crimes.

00:05:33.879 --> 00:05:36.030
That's Trump's personal lawyer.

00:05:36.030 --> 00:05:40.849
That would normally be a major sign that Trump
is guilty of something.

00:05:40.849 --> 00:05:45.780
But this is a rich former president and we
have a two tiered justice system in the United

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:46.780
States.

00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:50.979
In these at their core are crimes for which
you really need to prove state of mind.

00:05:50.979 --> 00:05:52.910
So this may go on for a long time.

00:05:52.910 --> 00:05:55.050
It will certainly trigger Trump.

00:05:55.050 --> 00:05:57.139
I don't think Trump ends up in prison.

00:05:57.139 --> 00:07:07.710
But let's talk about the reaction from the
former president next.

